luenced by Ijma’, which were a body of rulings on legal issues based on the consensus of scholars who had met to discuss specific cases. Despite the Caliphate’s claims to religious authority based on their links to the Prophet Muhammed, it was rare for direct rulings on legal matters to originate from the caliphal authorities. Alongside a developing legal code was the development of the Qudis, who were full time judiciary officials.<br>At least in the Umayyad period judges were "multicompetent state officials dealing with justice, police, tax, and finance issues."  Judges were appointed and were called Qadi.'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2598, 'temperature': 0.2})
